Let the equation of $f(x)$ be $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$. Thus, $g(x) = -ax^2-bx-c$ and $h(x)=a(-x)^2-b(-x)+c=ax^2+bx+c$. So, $h(x)$ and $f(x)$ are the same. To find the intersection points of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, we set $f(x) = -f(x)$, which gives us $2f(x) = 0$, or $f(x) = 0$. Since the graph of $f(x)$ is a parabola, it can intersect the x-axis at most twice. Therefore, $a = 2$.

To find the intersection points of $f(x)$ and $h(x)$, we set $f(x) = f(x)$, which gives us $f(x) = f(x)$. Since the graph of $f(x)$ is a parabola that opens upwards, it can only intersect itself once. Therefore, $b = 1$.

Thus, $10a + b = 10 \cdot 2 + 1 = \boxed{21}$

