To find the probability that it will snow at least once over the next three days, let's first calculate the probability that it will not snow at all over the three days. The probability that it will not snow on a given day is \(1 - \frac{2}{3} = \frac{1}{3}\). Since the probability of it not snowing on each of the three days is independent, the probability that it will not snow at all over the three days is \(\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^3 = \frac{1}{27}\).

The probability that it will snow at least once is the complement of the probability that it will not snow at all, which is \(1 - \frac{1}{27} = \frac{26}{27}\).

So, the probability that it will snow at least once during the three days is \(\boxed{\frac{26}{27}}

