The term \(a_n\) will be zero if \(|a_{n-2} - a_{n-3}| = 0\), which means \(a_{n-2} = a_{n-3}\). This implies that once the sequence contains two equal terms, it will continue to include zero because any term that multiplies by zero will result in zero.

So we need to check if the initial terms \((a_1, a_2, a_3)\) can eventually lead to two equal terms.

We can iterate over all possible ordered triples \((a_1, a_2, a_3)\) and generate the sequence up to \(a_n\) to check if a zero term appears. We'll use a brute-force approach with Python to do this.

